  <section id="why" class="prose">
    <h2>Why own the studio</h2>
    <p>A hosted creative tool can be a good way to get started. But if its prices, models or plans change, moving your projects elsewhere can be difficult. I wanted to keep my work and be able to change the tool as my needs changed.</p>
    <p>This studio does not train its own models. It gives you a workspace for your projects, references, saved takes, sound and public pages. You own the application and its stored work; the models are services you choose and pay for separately.</p>
    <p>App Composer brings separate apps together in one catalogue. Each app keeps its own source, data and release. Media Studio is an MIT-licensed Cloudflare Worker with its own D1 database, R2 bucket and deployment, so it can keep running without Composer. Your projects and media remain yours if a vendor changes an endpoint, although generation still depends on that vendor. This build ran into that limit firsthand.</p>
    <figure class="figure narrow">
      <img src={catalogue} width="1536" height="1024" alt="App Composer illustration: a catalogue hall in the centre with separate rooms for Guestbook and Showcase, each with its own data." loading="lazy" />
      <figcaption>The separate-app model from App Composer's playbook, 13 November 2026. Media Studio has its own storage and release. Illustration: App Composer playbook.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>There are trade-offs. A hosted service may be quicker to set up and offer editing or collaboration features this studio does not have. With your own studio, you manage the Cloudflare account, provider keys and releases, with help from your agent. The quality of generated work still depends on the model and your direction.</p>
    <p>You can keep your work, change the software and review an estimate before each paid request. Check your provider invoice for the actual charge.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="what" class="prose">
    <h2>What actually works</h2>
    <p>The initial release had a project library, uploads, stills and reference variations, masked edits, background removal, upscaling, two image-to-video models, sound, a browser mix, storyboards, scoped Composer access and deliberate publication. The 9:16 example was finished locally from the saved takes. Since then, the studio has added MiniMax H3 and a separate on-device storyboard renderer. The table records what was exercised in the original build, including the provider route that was blocked.</p>
    <figure class="figure"><img src={hero} width="1600" height="1000" alt="The Media Studio library for the Paper & Light project, with an assembled cut, sound and saved takes." loading="lazy" /><a href={hero} aria-label="Open full-size studio library screenshot">Open full-size image</a><figcaption>The working studio, with its assembled cut, sound and source takes.</figcaption></figure>
    <details class="production-details">
      <summary>See what this build could do</summary>
      <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <caption>Delivered capabilities and their real state on 2026/11/14, Australia/Melbourne (AEDT, UTC+11).</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Capability</th><th scope="col">What it is</th><th scope="col">State</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {capabilities.map(([name, detail, state]) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{name}</th><td>{detail}</td><td><span class:list={['tag', state.startsWith('Live') ? 'ok' : 'muted']}>{state}</span></td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
    </details>
    <p>The phrase "open-source Higgsfield-style studio" describes the kind of workspace, not feature parity. Higgsfield's models, camera controls, casting and node graph are proprietary and are not reproduced here. This studio calls vendor models; it does not train or host foundation models, and the providers used here charge for inference.</p>
    <h3>What the studio reuses</h3>
    <p>Get Ready checks the machine, Init saves the brief, and the agent handoff names the source and build steps. The studio also uses Composer's EmDash session and workspace permissions, with separate grants to read, edit, generate, publish and administer. Before a privileged request, it checks the current grant with Composer again. Levi's MIT Fungi projects informed parts of the build: Oyster hosted Prints for an earlier Composer capability run, and Darkroom's job engine informed the studio's engine. The studio does not include Darkroom itself. Its published catalogue accepts Fungi-gateway video models and Oyster-shaped references, which would need a second job authority for an independent Cloudflare studio. The studio repository records that assessment and its reasons.</p>
    <p>For this studio, I built audio parsing and waveforms, storyboards and bounded renders, signed links for render inputs, image variants, browser mixing and scoped Composer sessions. I also added editing for saved compositions and made the 9:16 derivative described below.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="brief" class="prose">
    <h2>From a sentence to a specification</h2>
    <p>Start in <a href="/init?start=media-studio">Init → Media Studio</a>. Choose what you want to make, the starting models, frame and clip length. This release also added sound model and bed-length defaults. Saving the draft does not provision services, buy credit or call a model.</p>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={init} width="1600" height="1000" alt="The Init form's Media Studio section: project name, what the studio should make, still and video model, default frame and clip length, and the saving note." loading="lazy" />
      <a href={init} aria-label="Open full-size Init form screenshot">Open full-size image</a>
      <figcaption>The Media Studio section of Init on the live page: what the studio should make, the still and video models, and the sound model and bed length added in this release. A saved draft carries defaults, never credentials.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>The draft becomes a portable specification. Its media section holds validated defaults; it does not provision services:</p>
    <pre><code>{`"mediaStudio": {
  "version": 2,
  "focus": "both",
  "imageModel": "studio-print",
  "videoModel": "hailuo-standard",
  "audioModel": "studio-score",
  "audioSeconds": 15,
  "aspect": "portrait",
  "durationSeconds": 6,
  "projectName": "Paper & Light"
}`}</code></pre>
    <p>Revision 2 added the sound defaults. A revision 1 draft still validates: its sound fields are filled in rather than rejected, so an older saved brief does not break. The studio rechecks every value on its own server; the spec never enables a provider or promises a model the studio has not qualified.</p>
    <p>You can export the spec and import the same choices later. "Copy agent handoff" gives your agent the pinned source, storage and secret names, and build plan. It also identifies the steps you handle: accounts, sign-in and spending approval. The handoff describes the work; the deployment showed it had been done.</p>
    <p><a class="inline-cta" href="/media-studio/tutorial#brief">The tutorial walks the same step with the screens and checkpoints →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section id="build" class="prose">
    <h2>How the agent builds it</h2>
    <p>Get Ready checks the local toolchain and the accounts the agent will need. Init's handoff pins the studio repository and an exact commit, then names the owner-controlled resources: a D1 database bound as <code>DB</code>, an R2 bucket bound as <code>MEDIA</code>, a Workers AI binding named <code>AI</code>, the owner passphrase and session secret, and an optional fal.ai key. The agent creates the owner's copy, applies the migrations in order, deploys, and reads back the live source identity.</p>
    <p>The studio's release script refuses to run from a dirty tree, from a branch that is not <code>main</code>, or from a commit that is not pushed. It runs the focused tests once, deploys, then fetches <code>/healthz</code> and fails the release unless the live revision equals the commit it just shipped. That is the difference between "deployed" and "I think it deployed".</p>
    <p>Two parts of the build needed particular care: paid requests and access to files during a render.</p>
    <h3>Keeping track of requests</h3>
    <p>Before a paid image, video, sound or assembly request is sent, the studio records the job and reserves its estimated cost. It checks concurrent requests against the same budget. If saving a finished result fails, the retained file can be saved again without making another generation request. When the provider outcome is unknown, the reservation stays in place until the job is reconciled; the studio does not automatically resubmit it.</p>
    <h3>How a provider render gets its files</h3>
    <p>At the time of this build, a composition took two to six saved clips, each up to 10.5 seconds, with a 60-second total and one saved audio bed. The current per-clip limit is 16 seconds. A provider render sends one composition and copies a successful result to the studio's bucket. The job stays recorded if you close the browser, and a failed result read does not silently start another render.</p>
    <p>The provider needs to read the participating files, and the studio's media endpoints require a current session. Rather than making a project public, the server signs one asset for one project with an expiry:</p>
    <pre><code>{`// src/lib/render-links.mjs — one asset, one project, thirty minutes
const payload = \`\${assetId}.\${projectId}.\${expires}\`;
const signature = await hmac(env.SESSION_SECRET, payload);
const url = new URL(\`/render-input/\${assetId}\`, origin);
url.searchParams.set("p", projectId);
url.searchParams.set("e", String(expires));
url.searchParams.set("s", signature);`}</code></pre>
    <p>The link cannot list other files, grant a session or expose a provider key, and it expires. The interface tells you before a render because the provider still receives the clips.</p>
    <p>The assembled frame follows the first keyframe. An earlier approach sorted portrait takes first to influence the frame, but that changed the chosen shot order and still produced 4:5 instead of 9:16. The next section describes the correction and the provider failure that followed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="use" class="prose">
    <h2>Making something real</h2>
    <p>I opened the studio from the Composer menu and started making. The normal route uses my existing sign-in, while the independent studio also has its own sign-in. Access to reading, editing, generating, publishing and administration is granted separately; provider keys and the studio passphrase stay off the browser.</p>
    <h3>Editing with a mask</h3>
    <p>Masked editing came later, using FLUX.1 Fill on fal. Pick a still, paint the area to change and describe what belongs there. The studio saves the source and a same-size, white-on-black mask separately, then saves the fill as a linked variant with its own hash. The original stays in the library.</p>
    <p>Outpainting uses the same tool deliberately: the studio builds a 25% larger canvas, centres the original, and masks the new border. Background removal is BiRefNet returning a transparent PNG; upscaling is Clarity at 2×. All three were exercised on the deployed studio, and all three save the result beside the source rather than over it.</p>
    <p>One implementation detail was not obvious: the provider's image fetcher refused the studio's signed HTTPS links, even though they were public and worked in a browser. The fix was to send the exact bytes as a data URI. That is also why the fill job now stores the source and mask hashes on the generation row: the provider never needs a durable public copy of either.</p>
    <h3>Making and saving sound</h3>
    <p>You can upload WAV, MP3 or M4A audio, or generate an instrumental bed with Studio Score and narration from your own script with Studio Voice. The studio saves each sound separately, records its duration, sample rate, channels, source and estimated cost, and draws the waveform from the audio file.</p>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={sound} width="1440" height="900" alt="The Create tab with Make sound selected: Studio Score, a written direction, a 17-second length, and an estimate before the request." loading="lazy" />
      <a href={sound} aria-label="Open full-size sound creation screenshot">Open full-size image</a>
      <figcaption>One paid request, estimated first. The length control arrived with the change below, so a bed can fit the cut instead of being padded afterwards.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p><a href="/media-studio/direct">Learn to direct a shot, plan sound and share a result in the creative field guide →</a></p>
    <p>The length matters. A 30-second bed under a 17-second cut left thirteen seconds of black and music. A 17-second bed produced a 17.02-second master with 16.79 seconds of picture: three takes, one bed and no long tail.</p>
    <h3>Putting the takes in order</h3>
    <p>A storyboard keeps your saved takes in order with their soundtrack. You can see the running length and the estimated cost before a provider render. The saved storyboard stays editable, and each render keeps the version it used.</p>
    <h3>Mixing the soundtrack</h3>
    <p>Music, narration and effects stay separate, so you can change the mix without losing a take. Choose up to four tracks, set their timing, trim, volume and fades, then save a new WAV. The browser makes the mix; the saved tracks and settings remain available if you want to rebuild it. The provider render uses that WAV rather than applying unsupported gain settings itself.</p>
    <p>Studio also has a separate on-device renderer for saved storyboards. It can make an MP4 with a saved mixed soundtrack in a compatible browser; the original clip audio is muted. This does not fix the provider’s blocked 9:16 compose route described below.</p>
    <h3>The 9:16 cut, after the bug</h3>
    <p>The previous release called a 768×960 file 9:16. It was 4:5, and the portrait-first sort changed the edit to suit the frame. Both were defects, and the claim was withdrawn. The replacement has two parts. The studio composes a deliberate 1080×1920 slate, generates one 720×1280 vertical slate video with Wan 2.5, and keeps those bytes so later renders reuse them. The selected takes then follow in the order chosen, composed into the slate's frame with the saved sound. No shot is re-sorted, stretched or silently regenerated.</p>
    <p>During this build, fal accepted new <code>ffmpeg-api</code> compose requests but returned a 403 permission error before reading the files. The studio keeps the request and offers <strong>Reconcile</strong> to check its result without submitting another paid job. A signed provider webhook confirmed the underlying model error. The 9:16 example on this page was instead finished locally with ffmpeg using the saved takes, sound and slate. It is 720×1280, 17.79 seconds, with 44.1 kHz stereo audio; the command and hashes are kept in the studio. The earlier 4:5 file remains in the project too.</p>
    <figure class="figure">
      <img src={storyboard} width="1440" height="900" alt="The Assemble tab showing a saved storyboard with three shots and an audio bed, a render button, and the assembled result below." loading="lazy" />
      <a href={storyboard} aria-label="Open full-size storyboard screenshot">Open full-size image</a>
